      "text": "To determine the measure of angle \\( P \\), we start by recognizing the properties of a circle and the angles formed by intersecting chords or secants. The key observation is that the sum of the angles formed by the intersection of two chords in a circle is related to the intercepted arcs.\n\nFirst, let's identify the intercepted arcs. The angles given in the problem (128°, 92°, 113°, and 111°) are formed by the intersection of chords in the circle. The central angle corresponding to each arc is twice the angle subtended at the center of the circle by that arc.\n\nLet's denote the central angles corresponding to the arcs as follows:\n- Let \\( \\angle APB = 128^\\circ \\), \\( \\angle CPD = 111^\\circ \\), \\( \\angle APE = 92^\\circ \\), and \\( \\angle BPD = 113^\\circ \\).\n\nSince the sum of the angles in a triangle is \\( 180^\\circ \\), we can use this property to find the measure of angle \\( P \\). However, we need to focus on the specific pairs of angles that are related to the central angles of the intercepted arcs.\n\nConsider the arc \\( AD \\). The central angle for the arc \\( AD \\) is \\( 180^\\circ - 113^\\circ = 67^\\circ \\). This central angle is formed by the angle \\( APD \\), so the angle \\( APD \\) is \\( 67^\\circ \\).\n\nSimilarly, consider the arc \\( BC \\). The central angle for the arc \\( BC \\) is \\( 180^\\circ - 111^\\circ = 69^\\circ \\). This central angle is formed by the angle \\( BPC \\), so the angle \\( BPC \\) is \\( 69^\\circ \\).\n\nNow, consider the sum of the angles around point \\( P \\). The angle \\( P \\) is the angle supplementary to the angle formed by the intersection of the secants, which is the average of the intercepted arcs. The intercepted arcs are \\( AD \\) and \\( BC \\), each of which subtends an angle of \\( 67^\\circ \\) and \\( 69^\\circ \\) at the center, respectively. However, the angle \\( P \\) is formed by the intersection of the chords \\( AC \\) and \\( BD \\), and it is related to the central angles of the intercepted arcs.\n\nSince the sum of the angles around point \\( P \\) is \\( 360^\\circ \\), we can use the fact that the angle \\( P \\) is the angle that completes the straight line with the other angles. The sum of the other angles is \\( 128^\\circ + 92^\\circ + 113^\\circ + 111^\\circ = 444^\\circ \\). Therefore, the angle \\( P \\) is:\n\n\\[\n360^\\circ - 444^\\circ + 180^\\circ = 180^\\circ\n\\]\n\nThus, the measure of angle \\( P \\) is \\(\\boxed{18}\\).",
