      "text": "To determine the measure of angle \\(P\\), we can use the property that the sum of the angles in a pentagon (or any polygon with an odd number of sides) is given by \\(180(n-2)\\) degrees, where \\(n\\) is the number of sides. For a pentagon, \\(n = 5\\), so the sum of the interior angles is \\(180(5-2) = 180 \\times 3 = 540\\) degrees.\n\nThe diagram provided shows five angles: \\(128^\\circ\\), \\(92^\\circ\\), \\(113^\\circ\\), \\(111^\\circ\\), and \\(P\\). We can use the fact that the sum of these angles is equal to \\(540^\\circ\\). Therefore, we can write the equation:\n\n\\[\n128^\\circ + 92^\\circ + 113^\\circ + 111^\\circ + P = 540^\\circ\n\\]\n\nFirst, we calculate the sum of the known angles:\n\n\\[\n128^\\circ + 92^\\circ + 113^\\circ + 111^\\circ = 444^\\circ\n\\]\n\nNext, we substitute this sum back into the equation and solve for \\(P\\):\n\n\\[\n444^\\circ + P = 540^\\circ\n\\]\n\nSubtracting \\(444^\\circ\\) from both sides gives:\n\n\\[\nP = 540^\\circ - 444^\\circ = 96^\\circ\n\\]\n\nThus, the measure of angle \\(P\\) is \\(\\boxed{96}\\).",
