      "text": "To solve for the measure of angle \\( P \\), we need to analyze the information given in the problem and use the properties of angles in a cyclic quadrilateral.\n\nFirst, let's identify the given angles and their relationships:\n- Angle \\( ABP = 128^\\circ \\)\n- Angle \\( CBE = 92^\\circ \\)\n- Angle \\( CDE = 113^\\circ \\)\n- Angle \\( AED = 111^\\circ \\)\n\nNotice that angles \\( ABP \\) and \\( CBE \\) are corollary angles on the same transversal line \\( BE \\). They are supplementary because they form a linear pair with angles \\( ABE \\) and \\( EBA \\), respectively. Similarly, angles \\( AED \\) and \\( CDE \\) are also supplementary.\n\nLet's denote the angle \\( P \\) as \\( \\angle APD \\). The sum of the angles in triangle \\( ABD \\) is \\( 180^\\circ \\). Therefore, we can write the equation for the sum of the angles in triangle \\( ABD \\):\n\n\\[\n\\angle BAD + \\angle ABD + \\angle ADB = 180^\\circ\n\\]\n\nWe know \\( \\angle BAD = \\angle ABP = 128^\\circ \\). Let \\( \\angle ABD = x \\) and \\( \\angle ADB = y \\). Then we have:\n\n\\[\n128^\\circ + x + y = 180^\\circ\n\\]\n\nThis simplifies to:\n\n\\[\nx + y = 52^\\circ\n\\]\n\nNext, we consider the cyclic quadrilateral \\( ABCD \\). The opposite angles of a cyclic quadrilateral sum to \\( 180^\\circ \\). Therefore, we have:\n\n\\[\n\\angle BAC + \\angle BCD = 180^\\circ\n\\]\n\nSince \\( \\angle BAC = \\angle ABC - \\angle BAE = 128^\\circ - \\angle BAE \\) and \\( \\angle BCD = \\angle BCD \\), we need to find the measure of \\( \\angle BAE \\). However, we can use the fact that the sum of angles around point \\( A \\) and \\( C \\) is \\( 360^\\circ \\).\n\nFrom the cyclic quadrilateral properties, we know:\n\n\\[\n\\angle ADB + \\angle CBA + \\angle CAB = 360^\\circ - 180^\\circ = 180^\\circ\n\\]\n\nSince \\( \\angle BCD = \\angle BCD \\) and \\( \\angle BAE = \\angle BAE \\), we can use the given angles to find the measure of \\( \\angle P \\).\n\nWe know:\n\n\\[\n\\angle ADB + \\angle BAC + \\angle ACB + \\angle CAD = 360^\\circ - 180^\\circ = 180^\\circ\n\\]\n\nGiven the angles around point \\( A \\) and \\( C \\), we can use the fact that the sum of angles in the triangle \\( ABD \\) is \\( 180^\\circ \\) and the sum of angles in the quadrilateral \\( ABCD \\) is \\( 360^\\circ \\).\n\nFinally, we can conclude that the measure of angle \\( P \\) is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{46^\\circ}\n\\]",
